      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Indeed, research suggests that students who defy traditional notions of linguistic proficiency by maintaining non-standard dialects may, in fact, have the cognitive flexibility and discipline needed to succeed in rigorous academic environments. Studies on dialect and executive function indicate that those who can consistently use and switch between dialects, including non-standard ones, may possess a high level of cognitive control, self-regulation, and even executive function skills, which could rival those of their standard English-speaking peers. This challenges the assumption that mastery of Standard English is the ultimate indicator of cognitive discipline.",
